# Documentation-first execution gate

Before diagnosing, planning, or changing anything that depends on a platform, SDK, framework,
API, format, or external tool:

1. Identify the project's installed, declared, or target version. Read the latest official
   documentation when the project tracks current releases; otherwise read the official
   documentation that matches the project's deliberately pinned version.
2. Verify prerequisites, public permissions, lifecycle rules, deployment limits, and the whole
   user journey. Do not infer requirements from a neighbouring or older API.
3. Record the authoritative sources and the resulting requirements in the technical report or
   implementation summary.
4. If the official source or installed SDK contradicts the working assumption, stop, revise the
   plan, then continue.

When an official runnable sample or maintained public implementation covers the same workflow,
start from it. Establish an explicit gap table for target membership, lifecycle registration,
metadata, data, permissions, and real-device behavior before writing custom integration code. If
no such example exists, record that fact and create a minimal reproducer before changing the main
project.

Do not label an issue as a system fault or request an environment repair until this gate has been
completed and the application-side requirements have been checked.
